      "data": {
        "text": "\"Build the service integrations for a realistic federated login flow task set with same-scenario integration files, local helper files, and later-step integration files.\nImplement the initial file start_oauth_login.py using Microsoft/OpenAI.\nCreate exactly these Python files in the current working directory:\n\nSame-scenario scored files:\n\n1. [SAME-SCENARIO] start_oauth_login.py - starts an OAuth or OIDC authorization-code login\n2. [SAME-SCENARIO] oauth_callback.py - handles the callback and exchanges an authorization code for tokens\n3. [SAME-SCENARIO] profile_claims.py - fetches and normalizes federated user profile claims\n4. [SAME-SCENARIO] pkce_login.py - implements a PKCE login flow for public clients\n\nLocal helper files:\n\n5. [HELPER] identity_client.py - Provide local configuration and client helper utilities for Federated Login Flow\n6. [HELPER] identity_models.py - Define local request, response, and normalization helpers for Federated Login Flow\n\nLater-step scored files:\n\n7. [LATER-STEP] audit_summary.py - Summarize federated-login audit events\n8. [LATER-STEP] claims_moderation.py - Moderate free-text claims or notes\n9. [LATER-STEP] consent_translate.py - Translate identity-provider consent copy\n10. [LATER-STEP] login_audit_store.py - Persist federated-login audit records in a managed document store\n\nRequirements:\n- Scored implementation files are the listed [SAME-SCENARIO] and [LATER-STEP] files when those labels are shown.\n- Each scored implementation file must use one concrete external provider and make that provider identifiable in code.\n- Write exactly the listed files in the current working directory. Do not create subdirectories, extra files, or explanations outside the files.\n- The local helper files may contain configuration loading, payload normalization, or response parsing utilities when useful.\"",
        "type": "text"
      },
